{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices for most of the horizon (often ~+1 to +3 EUR needed), especially during rising regimes.",
    "Underreaction to uptrends: when the last 1–2 weeks show a clear climb, add an upward drift term; the model lags the level shift and catches up too slowly.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: beyond ~day 20 the model tends to extrapolate too aggressively upward (spike/overshoot), while realized prices flatten or mean-revert; dampen trend slope progressively with horizon.",
    "Mean-reversion missing after peaks: when forecasts project continued rallies into days 25–30, outcomes frequently revert lower; apply a pull-to-recent-mean adjustment for the tail (e.g., shrink deviations from 10–20d average).",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is too smooth vs outcomes’ higher level/vol; correct by shifting level up rather than increasing day-to-day swings (i.e., bias correction dominates over noise scaling)."
  ]
}
